        "text_WhLmfj": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>In 1982, Japan’s Forestry Agency gave a name to something people had always done: <em>Shinrin-Yoku</em>, forest bathing. No water involved. You walk into the woods, slow down, and take in the atmosphere of the forest, the cool, green, resinous air that trees have been making for millions of years.</p><p>That air is not empty. Pines, cedars, and cypress release aromatic compounds called phytoncides, the same molecules that give a forest its smell. For more than four decades, researchers in Japan and around the world have followed people into the woods to study what that atmosphere means for the people standing in it. Japan now maintains dozens of certified forest trails built on that work.</p><p>BlissThera® is built on the same body of research, with one difference: <em>we bring the forest to you.</em> The tradition asks you to go to the Forest. We spent years engineering a way to make the forest’s atmosphere portable. Ancient practice. New technology.</p>",
            "text_style": "body"
          }
        },
        "button_EaXwQ9": {
